From cd291686cf12148d6d4027002debb9e4cff009ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 19:53:01 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] antinomie: add the host control channel and its shared-memory
 transport

Give the guest kernel a channel to the host that needs no network, no
device and no process in the guest. Host and guest share a bootstrap
page at a fixed guest-physical address (antinomie_boot_gpa=, by default
0x70000000). From it the guest publishes a control region with a
command queue, a reply queue and one telemetry ring per CPU
(antinomie_ring_kib=, a power of two from 256 KiB to 4 MiB; smaller
rings when memory is short).

guest.c owns that region. One ordered worker polls the command queue
every 10 ms and calls the handler registered for each opcode;
ant_send() lets any kernel code put a record in the calling CPU's ring
without allocating or sleeping. When the host asks senders to wait for
room instead of dropping, a sender that may sleep waits, on its CPU
and in turn with that CPU's other waiting senders (freed room goes to
them first), for as long as the host keeps reading that CPU's ring: it
gives up only when the host took nothing from it for the whole wait the
host set (at most 10 s). One that may not sleep spins for at most
200 microseconds. ant_send_room() and ant_send_waits() let a caller
that publishes in bulk pace itself. A fenced reattachment lets a host
that reconnected, or restored a snapshot, take the queues over without
replaying or losing a command. The worker is not a memory-reclaim
worker, because reading a terminal flushes the ordinary TTY workqueue.

control.c implements control protocol 1 on top of it (control_wire.h,
shared with the host): upload a file, launch a program in a pseudo
terminal of its own through a kernel user-mode helper with the
requested uid, gid and working directory (nothing runs in the guest to
do it), write to, read from and resize that terminal, signal its
process group, report what the kernel is (INFO) and power the guest
off.

Hooked kernel files:
- init/Kconfig and kernel/Makefile add CONFIG_ANTINOMIE and the
  kernel/antinomie directory.

The architecture reserves the bootstrap page from memblock
(ant_bootstrap_reserve()) before the kernel allocates memory, and keeps
the kernel's own placement off it; the x86-64 hooks are in the last
patch of the series.

Contract: INFO reports contract revision 17 (ANTC_REVISION), the
revision of the whole series, and CONFIG_ANTINOMIE_SOURCE_ID,
"<revision>:<series sha256>", which the build script sets and IKCONFIG
embeds. INFO first appeared in revision 9, the ring sizes and send
waits in revision 13 (transport ABI 8) and the reattachment fence in
revision 14 (transport ABI 9). A kernel without INFO answers it with
-EOPNOTSUPP.
